Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers on industrial I/O
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError() and .registers; pyModbusTCP typically returns None on failure and a list of ints on 
    │   success, requiring different error-handling logic; modbus-tk uses a modbus_tcp.TcpMaster with execute() and cst.READ_HOLDING_REGISTERS constants rather
    │   than named methods, requiring API rewrite; minimalmodbus is serial/RTU oriented (pyserial-based) and lacks a native TCP client concept, needing a 
    │   TCP-to-serial gateway or completely different connection setup; unit= keyword (slave id) handling differs across libraries and may need remapping; 
    │   connect()/close() semantics and exception types differ across these libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient to perform TCP-based holding-register reads/writes on a boiler controller. pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk both 
        provide comparable synchronous Modbus TCP master/client classes with read/write holding register methods, making them plausible substitutes for the 
        same functional role (industrial Modbus TCP communication). minimalmodbus is primarily a serial (RTU over pyserial) library with a very different 
        connection/read/write API (no unit=/address positional signature, no isError() result objects), so while it's a modbus library it would require 
        substantial glue code as the docstring itself acknowledges.

